Let us go through each of these four topics calmly, the way a senior developer would explain them to a junior joining the payments team.

First, CSRF stands for Cross-Site Request Forgery. The attack works like this: you log in to your bank in one browser tab, and your session cookie is now stored in the browser. In another tab you visit a malicious website. That website silently contains a hidden form or a piece of JavaScript that submits a request to your bank, for example a money transfer. Because the browser automatically attaches your bank's session cookie to any request going to the bank's domain, the bank sees a perfectly authenticated request and processes the transfer. You never clicked anything meaningful. The classic protection is the synchronizer token pattern: the server generates a secret random token, embeds it in the real form, and rejects any request that does not carry the matching token. A malicious site cannot read that token because of the browser's same-origin policy, so its forged request fails. In Spring Security, CSRF protection is enabled by default for session-based web applications. Here is what enabling it looks like:

```java
// CSRF protection ON (default) for a cookie/session based banking portal
http.csrf(csrf -> csrf
        .csrfTokenRepository(CookieCsrfTokenRepository.withHttpOnlyFalse()));
```

The important senior-level nuance is that CSRF only matters when the browser automatically sends credentials, which means cookies and sessions. A stateless REST API that authenticates with a JWT in the `Authorization` header is NOT vulnerable to CSRF, because the browser does not automatically attach that header for you — the JavaScript has to set it explicitly, and a malicious site cannot read your token to set it. That is why for stateless JWT APIs we safely disable CSRF:

```java
// Stateless REST API with JWT — CSRF disabled is safe and correct
http.csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
    .sessionManagement(sm -> sm.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS));
```

Second, CORS stands for Cross-Origin Resource Sharing. This is completely different from CSRF even though people confuse them. CORS is a browser safety rule. By default, JavaScript running on `https://app.mybank.com` is not allowed to read responses from `https://api.mybank.com` because they are different origins. The browser first sends a preflight `OPTIONS` request asking the server "are you willing to accept calls from this origin, with these methods and headers?" The server answers with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` and related headers. If the origin is allowed, the real request proceeds; if not, the browser blocks the JavaScript from seeing the response. In Spring you configure this with `@CrossOrigin` on a controller for quick cases, or globally with a `WebMvcConfigurer`:

```java
// Global CORS configuration for the whole service
@Configuration
public class CorsConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {
    @Override
    public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
        registry.addMapping("/api/**")
                .allowedOrigins("https://banking-portal.nationalbank.com")
                .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE");
    }
}
```

Third, Bean Validation. When a client sends JSON to a POST endpoint, we must reject bad data before it touches our business logic. We annotate the DTO fields with constraints such as `@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@Positive`, `@Min`, and `@Size`, and then put `@Valid` on the `@RequestBody` parameter. When validation fails, Spring throws a `MethodArgumentNotValidException`, which we convert into a clean 400 response using a `@RestControllerAdvice`. For example, a trade with a negative amount should never reach the matching engine:

```java
// DTO with validation constraints
public class TradeRequest {
    @NotBlank(message = "symbol is required")
    private String symbol;

    @Positive(message = "quantity must be greater than zero")
    private int quantity;

    @DecimalMin(value = "0.01", message = "amount must be positive")
    private BigDecimal amount;
    // getters / setters
}

// Controller triggers validation with @Valid
@PostMapping("/trades")
public ResponseEntity<TradeResponse> create(@Valid @RequestBody TradeRequest req) {
    return ResponseEntity.ok(service.executeTrade(req));
}
```

Fourth, Spring JDBC versus Spring Data JPA. Spring JDBC gives you `JdbcTemplate`, where you write the SQL by hand and map rows yourself. It is perfect for complex reporting queries where you want total control over the SQL. For example, a daily settlement summary:

```java
// Spring JDBC — hand-written SQL, full control
List<DailyReport> rows = jdbcTemplate.query(
    "SELECT txn_date, SUM(amount) AS total FROM trades WHERE status = ? GROUP BY txn_date",
    (rs, i) -> new DailyReport(rs.getDate("txn_date"), rs.getBigDecimal("total")),
    "SETTLED");
```

Spring Data JPA, by contrast, gives you a repository interface where you simply declare method names and Spring generates the query for you, backed by an ORM. It is ideal for standard CRUD. You never write SQL for simple lookups:

```java
// Spring Data JPA — method-name query, no SQL written
public interface TradeRepository extends JpaRepository<Trade, String> {
    List<Trade> findByAccountId(String accountId);
}
```

The practical rule a senior developer follows is: use Spring Data JPA for everyday entity CRUD, and drop down to Spring JDBC when you need a heavy analytical or reporting query that does not map cleanly to entities.

